```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Agent platforms running expensive multi-step agents (e.g., SWE-agent, tau-bench, AppWorld) that want to early-stop doomed trajectories to save compute costs. Only viable if the external monitor beats the agent's own self-confidence signal, which L0 fails to demonstrate (AUC delta -0.006, 95% CI [-0.011,-0.001], 96/96 seeds negative). The surviving weak form (beats generic difficulty, AUC +0.095) is insufficient for product adoption since self-confidence is already available for free from the agent and is modeled as a privileged direct readout of latent health.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark artifact in current form. L0 shows 2.32 step median lead time but only 42% coverage at 30% flag-rate (below pre-registered >0.5 bar). The 30% flag-rate implies high false positive cost—flagging nearly 1/3 of trajectories as doomed would waste successful runs. Real operational value requires L1 validation on real traces with real (miscalibrated) self-confidence, plus cost-benefit analysis of lead time vs. false positive rate. The monitor's edge collapses when abrupt/unforeseeable failures dominate (frac 0.2: 37% early, AUC 0.69), limiting real-world applicability where failures are not always foreshadowed.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Must beat agent self-confidence on REAL traces—L0 models self-confidence as privileged direct readout and monitor loses; published work suggests real LLM confidence is poorly calibrated, but L0 cannot settle this (requires L1 with real traces). [2] Coverage gap: 58% of failures not flagged early; production systems need higher recall. [3] Integration complexity vs. free self-confidence signal already emitted by agent—why deploy external monitor if agent already knows? [4] Flag-rate tuning: 30% is arbitrary; production requires careful trade-off between early-stopping savings and false-positive waste. [5] The "cheap monitor" (EWMA of tool-error/looping/info-gain) still adds latency, maintenance overhead, and another failure point.
RATIONALE: L0 EXP-0033 falsifies the strong claim (must beat BOTH baselines); monitor loses to self-confidence (AUC delta -0.006, 95% CI [-0.011,-0.001]). The surviving weak form (beats difficulty, AUC +0.095 CI [+0.087,+0.103], 96/96 seeds positive) is not product-viable because self-confidence is a free, privileged signal. Packet states: "The claim's strong form ('beats BOTH') is therefore FALSIFIED" and "The key L1 question... L0 cannot settle this. A real-trace L1 with real (miscalibrated) self-confidence is the only way to know." Negative result is actionable: teams should first measure their agent's self-confidence calibration; if well-calibrated, external monitor is redundant. Only if L1 shows monitor beats real miscalibrated confidence (and coverage improves beyond 42%) does product case emerge. The 0.115 oracle gap confirms monitor is not cheating, but also shows reconstruction penalty vs. direct readout.
```
